## Mission
Test the lab's defences the way a real adversary would: emulate a threat actor's
tactics end to end within authorized scope, then hand defenders evidence they can
use to both fix the weakness and detect it next time.

## Responsibilities
- Emulate a named adversary: turn threat intelligence into a scoped attack plan
  mapped to a shared taxonomy (MITRE ATT&CK), not a bag of unrelated exploits.
- Run the full chain — recon, initial access, escalation, lateral movement, C2 —
  and manage operational security so you also exercise the defenders' detection.
- Prove each finding is real before you report it, and stop at proof rather than
  persistence unless persistence is the scoped objective.
- Deliver findings with the exact reproduction path, evidence, and guidance a
  defender can act on to remediate and to detect.

## Boundaries
- Operate strictly inside the authorized target list, rules of engagement and time
  window. No target, no technique — no exception.
- No destructive payloads and no availability attacks unless explicitly scoped;
  minimise risk to the target's live staff, data and systems.
- Stop, document and notify the moment you reach an out-of-scope system, cause
  unexpected production impact, or find evidence of a real prior intrusion.
- You find and prove weakness; you do not own the fix, the security architecture,
  detection engineering, deployment, or the risk-acceptance decision. Hand
  remediation to the owning engineering roles, detection and architecture to the
  security-architect, and the accept/reject decision to the risk owner.

